\caption(First records, class-level intensity summaries and a raw image-summary distribution. The grid-summary API also computes traversal diagnostics used later; here only original intensity sums are displayed.)
\alttext(A histogram showing the distribution of total quantised intensity across 1797 images, with most totals around a few hundred units.)

> The method: prefer the most constrained next square

The board is a graph with one vertex per cell and an edge for each legal
knight displacement \((\pm1,\pm2)\) or \((\pm2,\pm1)\). Let
\(S_t\) be the cells already visited, including the current cell \(v_t\).
Legal candidates and their onward degrees are

$$
C_t=N(v_t)\setminus S_t,\qquad
\delta_t(u)=|N(u)\setminus S_t|,\qquad
v_{t+1}=\underset{u\in C_t}{\operatorname{argmin}}
\bigl(\delta_t(u),\operatorname{row}(u),\operatorname{column}(u)\bigr).
$$
\alttext(Choose among unvisited legal knight neighbours by the lexicographically smallest tuple of onward degree, row and column.)

A low-degree candidate has few alternatives and may become inaccessible
if deferred. That motivates the rule, but it is a local preference rather
than a proof that the remaining graph can still be covered. This
implementation breaks ties by the lowest row and then lowest column.
It does not use random choices, a secondary lookahead, restarts or
backtracking. The tie convention is part of the algorithm being measured.

The path stays legal and repeat-free by induction: the start is valid,
and each extension chooses a legal unvisited neighbour before marking it.
If its length reaches \(N=RC\), those distinct cells are a permutation
of the board. A complete path need not have a legal final-to-first edge;
`closed` checks that additional condition. At a dead end, the current
prefix has no extension, which does not establish impossibility from
the original starting square.

> Seeing successful and failed attempts

`warnsdorff_attempt()` retains the valid prefix and actual decision trace.
The original `warnsdorff_knight_tour()` uses the same loop and still returns
an empty vector on failure. The following two starts exhibit that difference.
The first step has two candidates of onward degree five; coordinate ties
select row one, column two.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/warnsdorff_attempts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  std::print("| start row | start column | visited | complete | closed | candidate probes | onward probes |\n"
      "| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto start : {std::pair<int,int>{0,0},{3,3}}) {
    const auto attempt = dm::algorithms::warnsdorff_attempt(8,8,start.first,start.second);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",start.first,start.second,attempt.path.size(),
        attempt.completed,attempt.closed,attempt.candidate_probes,attempt.degree_probes);
    layout.add(dm::algorithms::plot_knight_tour(attempt).title(std::format("{} visited: {}",attempt.path.size(),
        attempt.completed ? "complete" : "greedy dead end")).size(700,742));
  }
  std::print("\nFirst decisions from (0,0):\n\n{}\n",dm::algorithms::warnsdorff_attempt(8,8).trace.head(8).to_md());
  std::print("\nLast decisions from (3,3):\n\n{}\n",dm::algorithms::warnsdorff_attempt(8,8,3,3).trace.tail(3).to_md());
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1400,742).save_svg("build/warnsdorff_attempts.svg");
}
```
