\image(figures/warnsdorff_attempts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A complete path and a retained 62-cell failure prefix. Visit numbers start at zero; the start/end are green/red, knight edges blue and unvisited cells yellow. These board diagrams use Cartesian rows increasing upwards; input pixel columns retain their original row-major order.)
\alttext(Two numbered chequerboards linked by blue knight moves. The successful path visits all 64 cells; the failed path leaves two yellow cells unvisited and ends at a red marker.)

There are at most eight candidate directions and eight onward directions
per candidate. A decision therefore makes at most 72 direction probes.
There are at most \(N-1\) decisions, including any terminal stuck check,
so the recorded selection work satisfies

$$
\operatorname{candidate\_probes}+\operatorname{degree\_probes}
\le72(N-1).
$$
\alttext(The sum of candidate and onward-direction checks is at most seventy-two times one fewer than the number of board cells.)

The scan takes \(O(RC)\) time and \(O(RC)\) storage for visited flags,
path and diagnostics. The counters measure those direction checks, not
elapsed time or plotting work. Dimensions and cell budgets are checked
before allocation, and coordinate arithmetic avoids signed overflow.

> Starting-square sensitivity and a colour constraint

A knight move flips the parity of row plus column. Any complete path must
therefore alternate the board's two colours. On a 5 by 5 board one colour
has 13 cells and the other 12; a 25-vertex alternating path must start and
end in the larger colour. A minority-colour start is impossible regardless
of heuristic. This necessary condition alone says nothing conclusive about
other starts.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/warnsdorff_starts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| board | starts | completed paths | closed tours |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const int n : {5,6,8}) {
    const auto profile = dm::algorithms::warnsdorff_start_profile(n,n);
    const auto counts = profile | dm::summarise(dm::agg::sum("completed","complete"),dm::agg::sum("closed","closed"));
    std::print("| {} x {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,n,profile.nrows(),counts.double_at("complete",0),counts.double_at("closed",0));
  }
  std::print("\n5 by 5, by starting colour:\n\n{}\n",(dm::algorithms::warnsdorff_start_profile(5,5) |
      dm::group_by("colour") | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("starts"),dm::agg::sum("completed","complete"))).to_md());
  const auto profile = dm::algorithms::warnsdorff_start_profile(8,8);
  const auto good = profile.filter([](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("completed")!=0; });
  const auto bad = profile.filter([](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("completed")==0; });
  std::print("\nFailed 8 by 8 starts:\n\n{}\n",bad.select({"start_row","start_column","visited_cells"}).to_md());
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(good.get_column("start_column").values(),good.get_column("start_row").values(),"",dm::RGB{22,163,74},5)
      .points(bad.get_column("start_column").values(),bad.get_column("start_row").values(),"",dm::RGB{220,38,38},7);
  for (std::size_t i=0;i<bad.nrows();++i) plot.text(bad.double_at("start_column",i)+0.18,bad.double_at("start_row",i)+0.2,
      std::format("{:.0f}",bad.double_at("visited_cells",i)),dm::RGB{15,15,15},12);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  plot.title("8 x 8 starts under coordinate tie breaking").x_label("starting column").y_label("starting row")
      .x_limits(-0.5,7.5).y_limits(-0.5,7.5).size(800,842).save_svg("build/warnsdorff_starts.svg");
}
```
