\image(figures/warnsdorff_starts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Green starts complete a tour; red starts get stuck, with labels giving their visited-cell counts. The profile enumerates starts under one deterministic policy, not a probability of success under random tie breaking.)
\alttext(An eight-by-eight array of starting positions with 61 green successes and three red failures labelled 60, 62 and 56.)

The 8 by 8 profile contains six closed tours. Rotating the vertex order of
any such cycle yields a valid complete tour starting at any square. Thus
all 64 starts have a solution, even though this greedy policy fails at
three of them. Its coordinate tie rule also breaks geometric symmetry:
rotating a start need not rotate the algorithm's choices. Results from a
fixed move-order tie rule or a randomised rule need not equal this table.
Enumerating all starts costs \(O((RC)^2)\), so that helper has a separate
explicit start-count limit.

> Applying the complete path as a pixel permutation

For image vector \(v\), let \(\pi_i\) be the row-major index of the
\(i\)-th visited cell. A complete path gives a permutation matrix \(P\)
and readout \(u_i=v_{\pi_i}\). Since every original cell appears once,

$$
u=Pv,\qquad \mathbf1^T P=\mathbf1^T,\qquad
\sum_i u_i=\sum_i v_i.
$$
\alttext(A complete tour permutes image values. Every column of its permutation matrix contains one unit entry, so the sum of intensities is unchanged.)

The actual coordinate mapping also gives an inverse: put each readout
value back at its recorded row-major index. No image value is averaged or
lost. The helper checks actual legality, uniqueness and coverage before
applying the path; a partial prefix is rejected instead of silently
dropping unvisited pixels. In generic floating-point data, reordered sums
can differ by roundoff. These small integer intensities sum exactly.

Adjacent-value total variation does depend on order:
\(\operatorname{TV}(v)=\sum_{i=1}^{63}|v_i-v_{i-1}|\). A knight edge
has geometric length \(\sqrt5\) pixel spacings, while row-major readout
usually moves one cell and occasionally wraps a row. Warnsdorff's degree
rule uses none of the pixel values, so it supplies no minimum-variation
or compression guarantee.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/warnsdorff_readout.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits(); auto columns = digits.columns();
  columns.erase(std::remove(columns.begin(),columns.end(),"digit"),columns.end());
  const auto attempt = dm::algorithms::warnsdorff_attempt(8,8);
  const auto values = dm::algorithms::knight_tour_grid_values(digits,0,columns,attempt);
  std::print("First image, two readout orders:\n\n{}\n",values.head(10).to_md());
  auto summary = dm::algorithms::knight_tour_grid_summary(digits,columns,attempt);
  summary.add_column("digit",digits.get_column("digit").values());
  std::print("\n{}\n",(summary | dm::group_by("digit") | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("images"),
      dm::agg::mean("row_major_variation","mean_row_major_tv"),dm::agg::mean("tour_variation","mean_tour_tv"))).to_md(4));
  std::print("\n{}\n",(summary | dm::summarise(dm::agg::mean("row_major_variation","mean_row_major_tv"),
      dm::agg::mean("tour_variation","mean_tour_tv"),dm::agg::min("sum_difference","min_sum_delta"),
      dm::agg::max("sum_difference","max_sum_delta"))).to_md(6));
  summary = summary | dm::mutate("variation_difference",[](const auto& r) { return r.get_double("tour_variation")-r.get_double("row_major_variation"); });
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(values.get_column("step").values(),values.get_column("row_major_value").values(),
      "row major",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2).line(values.get_column("step").values(),values.get_column("tour_value").values(),"knight tour",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("One image, unchanged values").x_label("readout step").y_label("pixel intensity").y_limits(0,20).size(750,480));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::hist(summary.get_column("variation_difference").values(),35,"images")
      .abline_v(0,dm::RGB{220,38,38},1).title("Change in adjacent-value variation")
      .x_label("tour TV minus row-major TV").y_label("images").size(750,480));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1500,480).save_svg("build/warnsdorff_readout.svg");
}
```
