\image(figures/warnsdorff_readout.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Two readouts of image zero and dataset-wide variation summaries. Positive differences mean the tour order has larger adjacent-value variation. All 1797 original integer intensity sums are preserved exactly.)
\alttext(A pair of intensity sequences for the same image beside a histogram showing tour readout usually has larger total variation than row-major order.)

Mean total variation rises from 271.00223 in row-major order to 381.55426
along this knight tour. The sum-difference range is exactly zero. These
results check both aspects of the derivation: a permutation preserves
the values and their sum, while changing adjacency can substantially
change a sequence statistic. They do not establish that either order
is universally best for a downstream coding or learning task.

> Conclusion

Minimum onward degree gives a cheap local choice and a legal simple path.
Completion, closure and failure are separate outcomes, and deterministic
ties materially affect them. Closed tours found by the same experiment
certify that all standard-board starts are solvable, exposing the three
greedy failures as limitations of the rule. Applied to digit measurements,
a complete tour is an invertible reordering with preserved intensity sums;
its increased total variation makes clear that geometric coverage and
image-value smoothness are different objectives.

> References

- H. C. von Warnsdorf (1823). *Des Rösselsprunges einfachste und allgemeinste Lösung*.
  Digitised original: https://books.google.com/books?id=w5FZAAAAYAAJ
- S. L. Marateck (2008). *How good is the Warnsdorff's knight's tour heuristic?*
  https://arxiv.org/abs/0803.4321
  The paper studies fixed move-order ties; this report uses explicit coordinate ties.
- scikit-learn, *Optical recognition of handwritten digits dataset*.
  https://scikit-learn.org/stable/datasets/toy_dataset.html#optical-recognition-of-handwritten-digits-dataset
